<div class="tab {{tabs.battlefields.cssClass}}" data-group="primary" data-tab="battlefields">

{{! Its own tab rather than a panel inside Combat. A Battlefield is a thing the whole
    table is standing in rather than a number on one character, and the Combat tab is
    already a column of this character's own tracking values.

    Every rule here is about this character alone. What the rest of the map is like is
    the table's - nothing in this system has Squares to ask - so what is on this tab is
    set by the player, or by whatever else the table decides.

    Laid out as two rows of three pickers first - where you are, then what the sky and
    the light are doing - so the two read as one block. Then what the Square is like,
    then Cover, then the buttons, and everything that is only for reading last, folded
    away under one heading. }}
  <section class="panel">
    <h3 class="panel-title">
      Battle Environment
      <span class="panel-hint">The land you are standing on</span>
    </h3>

    <div class="stat-grid battlefield-row">
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="What you are standing on. An Environment may cover a whole Battlefield or only part of one - which Squares are which is the table's, and stepping into them is picking one here.">
        <em>Environment</em>
        <select name="system.battlefield.environment">
          {{#each environments as |environment|}}
          <option value="{{environment.id}}" {{#if environment.chosen}}selected{{/if}}>{{environment.name}}</option>
          {{/each}}
        </select>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="How far off the ground you are. The first three ranks are layered above the Battle Environment below, which stops reaching you while you are up there - it is still what you would land in.">
        <em>High Environment</em>
        <select name="system.battlefield.highEnvironment" data-dtype="Number">
          <option value="0" {{#unless high.rank}}selected{{/unless}}>On the ground</option>
          {{#each highEnvironments as |sky|}}
          <option value="{{sky.rank}}" {{#if sky.chosen}}selected{{/if}}>{{sky.name}} ({{sky.rank}})</option>
          {{/each}}
        </select>
      </div>
      {{#if ground}}
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="{{ground.note}}">
        <em>Ground Hardness</em>
        <select name="system.battlefield.groundHardness" data-dtype="Number">
          {{#each ground.ranks as |rank|}}
          <option value="{{rank.rank}}" {{#if rank.chosen}}selected{{/if}}>Rank {{rank.rank}} &middot; {{rank.material}}</option>
          {{/each}}
        </select>
        {{#if ground.shifted}}
        {{! What the Square's own Qualities leave it at. Shown only when it differs from the
            Rank picked above, and inside the same tile so the row keeps its three. }}
        <small class="stat-after" data-tooltip="{{ground.shifted.note}}">After Qualities: Rank {{ground.shifted.rank}}</small>
        {{/if}}
      </div>
      {{/if}}
    </div>

    {{#if high.note}}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">{{high.note}}</p>
    {{/if}}

    {{#if environmentNow.effect}}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">{{environmentNow.effect}}</p>
    {{/if}}

    {{#if breath}}
    {{! Held Breath, which only an Unbreathable Environment has. The stacks are shown
        rather than edited: they are handed out by one Check and taken by the Combat Round
        and by Health Thresholds, and a field to type in beside all that is a fifth way
        for the number to be wrong. }}
    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="{{breath.note}}">
        <em>Held Breath</em><span>{{breath.held}}</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    {{#if breath.canRoll}}
    <div class="maneuver-list">
      <button type="button" class="dbu-clash-button" data-action="holdBreath"
              data-tooltip="A Survival Check, made on entering. Every Difficulty the Dice Score reaches is a stack of Held Breath.">
        Hold Breath
      </button>
    </div>
    {{/if}}
    {{/if}}
  </section>

  {{! Battle Weather and Light Level on one row: both are what the sky is doing to you,
      and three tiles here line up with the three above. }}
  <section class="panel">
    <h3 class="panel-title">
      Battle Weather
      <span class="panel-hint">Set by you, or by an effect</span>
    </h3>

    <div class="stat-grid battlefield-row">
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="Which Battle Weather you are standing in. Its effects only apply to you while you are in the Squares it covers, and which Squares those are is the table's.">
        <em>Weather</em>
        <select name="system.battlefield.weather.id">
          <option value="" {{#unless weatherNow.id}}selected{{/unless}}>Clear</option>
          {{#each weathers as |weather|}}
          <option value="{{weather.id}}" {{#if weather.chosen}}selected{{/if}}>{{weather.name}}</option>
          {{/each}}
        </select>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="How bad it is. Each Tier gains the effects of the earlier Tiers, and a number written (WT) is multiplied by this.">
        <em>Weather Tier</em>
        <select name="system.battlefield.weather.tier" data-dtype="Number">
          {{#each weatherTiers as |tier|}}
          <option value="{{tier.tier}}" {{#if tier.chosen}}selected{{/if}}>{{tier.name}} ({{tier.tier}})</option>
          {{/each}}
        </select>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="What the light where you are standing is doing to you. Too bright and too dark cost the same: the table is symmetrical about Normal.">
        <em>Light Level</em>
        <select name="system.battlefield.lightLevel" data-dtype="Number">
          {{#each lightLevels as |level|}}
          <option value="{{level.value}}" {{#if level.chosen}}selected{{/if}}>{{level.label}}</option>
          {{/each}}
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>

    {{#if weatherNow.effect}}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">{{weatherNow.effect}}</p>
    {{/if}}

    {{#if lightLevel.effect}}
    {{! What the chosen Level is doing, in the rulebook's own words. Drawn only when it is
        doing something: Normal says "no effect", and a row repeating that is a row to
        read past. }}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">{{lightLevel.effect}}</p>
    {{/if}}
  </section>

  {{! The Environmental Qualities of the Square. "Applied on a Square-by-Square basis and
      ultimately decided by the ARC" - so they are ticked here, except the ones the
      Environment's own file brings with it, which are shown and not tickable. }}
  <section class="panel">
    <details class="condition-group" data-section="qualities"
             {{#if openSections.qualities}}open{{/if}}>
      <summary class="panel-title">
        Environmental Qualities
        <span class="panel-note">{{qualitySummary}}</span>
      </summary>

      <ul class="condition-list">
        {{#each qualities as |quality|}}
        <li class="condition {{#if quality.active}}condition-active{{/if}}">
          <button type="button" class="condition-toggle" data-action="toggleQuality"
                  data-quality="{{quality.id}}" data-tooltip="{{quality.text}}"
                  aria-pressed="{{#if quality.active}}true{{else}}false{{/if}}"
                  {{#if quality.fixed}}disabled{{/if}}>
            <span class="condition-mark">{{#if quality.active}}&#10003;{{/if}}</span>
            <span class="condition-name">{{quality.name}}</span>
          </button>
        </li>
        {{/each}}
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h3 class="panel-title">
      Cover
      <span class="panel-hint">Turned on by you, for as long as it lasts</span>
    </h3>

    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="A Feature between you and whoever is attacking you. Nothing here knows where anything is standing, so this is yours to turn on and off.">
        <em>Behind cover</em>
        <input type="checkbox" name="system.battlefield.cover.active"
               {{#if system.battlefield.cover.active}}checked{{/if}}/>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="What the Feature you are behind is made of. Twice its Hardness Value comes off the Damage you would suffer, and the Value is worked out from your base Tier of Power.">
        <em>Hardness</em>
        <select name="system.battlefield.cover.rank" data-dtype="Number">
          {{#each hardnessRanks as |hardness|}}
          <option value="{{hardness.rank}}" {{#if hardness.behind}}selected{{/if}}>Rank {{hardness.rank}} &middot; {{hardness.material}}</option>
          {{/each}}
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>

    {{#if cover.note}}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">{{cover.note}}</p>
    {{/if}}

    {{! The one case the rule takes itself back in. Said beside the toggle, because that is
        where somebody deciding whether to leave it on is looking. }}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">
      Cover does not apply if the Attacking Maneuver has an Area of Effect that includes
      the Feature as a target. Areas are the table's here: turn this off for that attack.
    </p>
  </section>

  {{! Battle Terrain: throwing an attack at a Feature, and the other direction. Two to a
      row, so four make a square rather than a column. }}
  <section class="panel">
    <div class="battlefield-buttons">
      {{#each featureAttacks as |attack|}}
      <button type="button" class="dbu-clash-button" data-action="attackFeature"
              data-item-id="{{attack.itemId}}"
              data-tooltip="Throw it at a Feature rather than at a Character. No Strike Roll and no Wound Roll: the card says the Damage.">
        {{attack.name}}
      </button>
      {{/each}}

      {{! Most collisions in these rules are not a card this system opened - walked into,
          fallen onto, pushed by something that never rolled - and this is where a player
          says one happened. }}
      <button type="button" class="dbu-clash-button" data-action="takeCollision"
              data-tooltip="You hit something. Pick what it was made of and the damage is worked out from your base Tier of Power.">
        Receive Collision Damage
      </button>
    </div>
  </section>

  {{! Everything that is only for reading, folded away under one heading. Nothing inside
      sets anything; the pickers above are where the numbers come from. }}
  <section class="panel">
    <details class="condition-group" data-section="information"
             {{#if openSections.information}}open{{/if}}>
      <summary class="panel-title">Information</summary>

      {{! Battle Weather, for reading: the three Tiers, and the rules that hold whichever
          one is in play. }}
      <details class="condition-group" data-section="weather"
               {{#if openSections.weather}}open{{/if}}>
        <summary class="panel-title">Battle Weather</summary>

        <ul class="quality-list">
          {{#each weatherTiers as |tier|}}
          <li class="quality">
            <strong>{{tier.name}} <span class="quality-mark">Tier {{tier.tier}}</span></strong>
            <span>{{tier.text}}</span>
          </li>
          {{/each}}
        </ul>

        <ul class="quality-list">
          {{#each weatherRules as |rule|}}
          <li class="quality">
            <strong>{{rule.name}}</strong>
            <span>{{rule.text}}</span>
          </li>
          {{/each}}
        </ul>
      </details>

      {{! The Environment frame, for reading. Both rules are about Squares, which is to
          say both are the table's. }}
      <details class="condition-group" data-section="environments"
               {{#if openSections.environments}}open{{/if}}>
        <summary class="panel-title">Environments</summary>

        <ul class="quality-list">
          {{#each environmentRules as |rule|}}
          <li class="quality">
            <strong>{{rule.name}}</strong>
            <span>{{rule.text}}</span>
            {{#if rule.note}}<em>{{rule.note}}</em>{{/if}}
          </li>
          {{/each}}
        </ul>
      </details>

      {{! Hardness. Two numbers wearing one name, and the one that matters at the table is
          not stored anywhere - the Value is worked out against whoever is taking the
          damage, so the same wall costs two characters two different numbers. }}
      <details class="condition-group" data-section="hardness"
               {{#if openSections.hardness}}open{{/if}}>
        <summary class="panel-title">
          Hardness
          <span class="panel-note">Your Values, at base Tier {{baseTierOfPower}}</span>
        </summary>

        {{! Said first, and said in the rules' own words. The Value is not something the
            Collision Damage is worked out from - it is the Collision Damage. }}
        <p class="tab-placeholder">
          <strong>{{collisionDamage}}</strong>
        </p>

        <p class="tab-placeholder">
          The Hardness Rank is what a Feature is made of, and is also its Life Points. The
          Hardness Value is what hitting it costs - twice the Rank a base Tier of Power,
          except Rank 0, which is 1(bT). The Values below are yours: they scale with the
          base Tier of Power of whoever suffers the Collision Damage, so another character
          hitting the same wall takes a different number.
        </p>

        <ul class="quality-list">
          {{#each hardnessRanks as |hardness|}}
          <li class="quality">
            <strong>Rank {{hardness.rank}} <span class="quality-mark">{{hardness.value}} Damage to you</span></strong>
            <span>{{hardness.text}}</span>
          </li>
          {{/each}}
        </ul>
      </details>

      {{! Feature Qualities. A Quality belongs to a Feature and a Feature is the ARC's, so
          there is nothing here to set. Six of them are asked about when Collision Damage
          is applied, which is the one moment any of this reaches a character. }}
      <details class="condition-group" data-section="featureQualities"
               {{#if openSections.featureQualities}}open{{/if}}>
        <summary class="panel-title">Feature Qualities</summary>

        <p class="tab-placeholder">
          The material is what a Square, Feature or other object is made out of. Generally
          the ARC decides the material and picks an appropriate Hardness Rank for it - and
          some Features have Qualities on top of that, decided the same way.
        </p>

        <ul class="quality-list">
          {{#each featureQualities as |quality|}}
          <li class="quality">
            <strong>{{quality.name}}{{#if quality.onCollision}} <span
              class="quality-mark" data-tooltip="Offered when Collision Damage is applied, because that is the moment it does something.">asked on collision</span>{{/if}}</strong>
            <span>{{quality.text}}</span>
            {{#if quality.note}}<em>{{quality.note}}</em>{{/if}}
          </li>
          {{/each}}
        </ul>
      </details>
    </details>
  </section>
</div>
